<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>SshWarden.OAuth</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    OAuth 2.1 for any authorization server, and now the only adapter this repository ships.

    There used to be a second one, wired to a sibling project's packages so that project's tokens
    were read by its own code. It is gone, and the reason it went is worth keeping: what an
    authorization server hands a resource server is a JWT, a JWKS document and an RFC 9728 resource
    identifier. All three are standards, this assembly already speaks all three, and pointing it at
    that server needed one config line naming the claim it spells differently. A second reader of
    the same standards is a second thing to keep correct, and it put another project's packages in
    the dependency graph of a server whose selling point is that it validates somebody else's
    tokens.

    So: Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer, which every .NET reader already knows, doing
    OpenID Connect discovery against the configured issuer. What it does not do is RFC 9728 - that
    document is the resource server's own, not the authorization server's, and it is sixty lines
    written here rather than a dependency.

    Nothing in this repository requires this assembly either. It fills ISshWardenAuthenticator,
    which is the seam, and a deployment references it or writes its own.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../SshWarden.Mcp/SshWarden.Mcp.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authentication.JwtBearer" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
